  writings: [
    { title: "Summa Theologiae", original: "Summa Theologiae", genre: "Systematic theology", year: "1265–1273, unfinished", language: "Latin", note: "About 1.5 million words in three parts (God; the moral life; Christ and the sacraments), each of ~3,000 articles. Written, he says, for beginners. The *Supplementum* was assembled after his death from earlier work.", translation: "The Fathers of the English Dominican Province (1911, public domain) is the standard free text; the Latin/English Blackfriars edition (61 vols., Cambridge) is the scholarly one; Freddoso's ongoing translation is the best modern English", publisher: "Christian Classics / Cambridge UP", free: "Full text at newadvent.org and isidore.co", difficulty: "Do not read front to back. Take one question at a time.", order: 3 },
    { title: "Summa contra Gentiles", original: "Summa contra Gentiles / Liber de veritate catholicae fidei", genre: "Apologetic theology", year: "1259–1265", language: "Latin", note: "Four books arguing the faith without appeal to Scripture where reason can reach — traditionally said to be for missionaries to Muslims and Jews, more likely a general defence of Christian truth. More continuous prose than the Summa Theologiae.", translation: "Anton Pegis et al., *On the Truth of the Catholic Faith*", publisher: "University of Notre Dame Press", free: "dhspriory.org", difficulty: "Easier to read continuously than the Summa Theologiae", order: 4 },
    { title: "The Corpus Christi Office and Hymns", original: "Officium de festo Corporis Christi", genre: "Liturgy and hymnody", year: "1264", language: "Latin", note: "Commissioned by Urban IV. Contains *Pange lingua* (whose last two stanzas are the *Tantum ergo*), *Adoro te devote*, *Lauda Sion*, *Verbum supernum prodiens* (whose last stanzas are the *O salutaris hostia*), and *Sacris solemniis* (*Panis angelicus*). The greatest theologian of the Church also wrote its best Eucharistic hymns.", translation: "Sung in Latin everywhere; Gerard Manley Hopkins's version of *Adoro te devote* is the finest English one", publisher: "In every hymnal", free: "Yes, universally", difficulty: "Easy, and the best place to start with him", order: 1 },
    { title: "Catena Aurea", original: "Catena Aurea", genre: "Scripture commentary", year: "1262–68", language: "Latin", note: "A 'golden chain' of patristic commentary on all four Gospels, verse by verse — Thomas as editor rather than author, and a superb way to read the Fathers.", translation: "The 19th-century Oxford translation edited by John Henry Newman", publisher: "Baronius Press", free: "Public domain", difficulty: "Easy; use it as a companion to the daily Gospel", order: 2 },
    { title: "Disputed Questions on Truth", original: "Quaestiones disputatae de veritate", genre: "Disputed questions", year: "1256–59", language: "Latin", note: "Twenty-nine questions from his first Paris regency — on truth, knowledge, conscience, providence, the will. Far more expansive than the parallel Summa articles.", translation: "Mulligan, McGlynn & Schmidt, 3 vols.", publisher: "Hackett", free: "dhspriory.org", difficulty: "Hard", order: 6 },
    { title: "Commentary on the Gospel of John", original: "Lectura super Ioannem", genre: "Scripture commentary", year: "1270–72", language: "Latin", note: "Thomas thought of himself first as a Master of the Sacred Page — a Scripture teacher. This is the finest of his biblical commentaries and shows a side of him the Summa does not.", translation: "Fabian Larcher & James Weisheipl", publisher: "Catholic University of America Press", free: "dhspriory.org", difficulty: "Moderate", order: 5 },
    { title: "On Being and Essence", original: "De ente et essentia", genre: "Philosophical treatise", year: "c. 1252", language: "Latin", note: "An early work of about thirty pages containing the core of his metaphysics — the real distinction between essence and existence. Short enough to read in an evening and dense enough to spend a life on.", translation: "Armand Maurer", publisher: "PIMS", free: "Yes", difficulty: "Short but hard", order: 7 },
    { title: "Commentaries on Aristotle", original: "Sententia libri Ethicorum, In Metaphysicam, In De anima, etc.", genre: "Philosophical commentary", year: "1268–1272", language: "Latin", note: "Twelve of them, written at extraordinary speed during his second Paris regency, using Moerbeke's new translations from the Greek. Still used by Aristotle scholars who are not Christians.", translation: "Various, mostly CUA Press and Dumb Ox Books", publisher: "Various", free: "dhspriory.org", difficulty: "Hard; for readers of Aristotle", order: 8 },
  ],

  writingsNotes: "He wrote or dictated roughly eight and a half million words in under twenty years. Almost everything is online free in Latin and English at the Dominican House of Studies site (dhspriory.org / isidore.co). Practical route in: the Eucharistic hymns, then the *Catena Aurea* alongside the daily Gospel, then single questions of the *Summa* — not chapters of it. Read Chesterton or Feser first if you want a map. The *Shorter Summa* (*Compendium theologiae*) is his own attempt at a condensed version and is genuinely short.",

