    conversion: "Not a single instant but a sequence: the leper's kiss dissolved his old disgust; San Damiano's crucifix gave him a mission he first misread literally; only in 1208, hearing the Gospel's call to itinerant, propertyless preaching, did the whole shape become clear.",

    anecdotes: [
      { title: "The kiss of the leper", text: "Riding one day, Francis saw a leper on the road and felt the old revulsion rise in him. He got off his horse anyway, put money in the man's hand, and kissed it. When he looked back, he said, the leper was gone.", source: "Francis's own Testament", legend: false },
      { title: "Stripping before the bishop", text: "Summoned by his furious father before the Bishop of Assisi to answer for selling the cloth, Francis took off all his clothes on the spot, handed them back, and said: 'Until now I called Pietro Bernardone my father. From now on I can say only, Our Father who art in heaven.'", source: "Thomas of Celano, First Life", legend: false },
      { title: "Preaching to the birds", text: "Passing a flock of birds on the road, Francis stopped his companions and preached to them — that they should praise God, who clothes and feeds them — and the birds, it's said, did not fly off until he had finished and blessed them.", source: "Thomas of Celano, First Life; Fioretti", legend: true },
      { title: "The wolf of Gubbio", text: "A wolf terrorizing Gubbio was, by legend, tamed by Francis with a simple bargain: the town would feed it, and it would harm no one again. The wolf reportedly lived out its days as the town's pet.", source: "The Fioretti (a later 14th-century collection, not a contemporary source)", legend: true },
      { title: "The first Christmas crèche", text: "At Greccio in 1223, wanting people to feel the poverty of Bethlehem with their own senses rather than just hear it preached, Francis arranged a live manger scene with real animals in a cave outside town — the origin of the Christmas nativity scene tradition still kept worldwide.", source: "Thomas of Celano, First Life, ch. 30", legend: false },
      { title: "Brother Fire", text: "Undergoing an agonizing cauterization of his diseased eyes near the end of his life, Francis addressed the branding iron itself as 'Brother Fire,' asking it to be courteous to him since he had always loved it — and, his companions reported, he did not flinch when it was applied.", source: "Thomas of Celano, Second Life", legend: false },
      { title: "\"Preach the Gospel at all times\"", text: "Setting out with a young friar to preach in town, Francis walked the streets in silence, greeting people and going about ordinary tasks, then turned back toward the friary. When the friar asked when they would preach, Francis answered that they already had — 'preach the Gospel at all times; when necessary, use words.'", source: "Attributed in later Franciscan tradition; not found in the earliest biographies, and its wording is likely apocryphal though the sentiment is consistent with his teaching", legend: true },
    ],

    phenomena: ["The stigmata, received on La Verna in September 1224 — the first documented case in Christian history, witnessed by Brother Leo, who wrote of it in his own hand on a scrap of parchment Francis had given him"],
    miracles: "Numerous healing miracles are recorded by Thomas of Celano and in the canonization process, but Francis's own writings barely mention the miraculous at all — his own emphasis, and the Church's, falls far more on his poverty and his conformity to Christ than on wonders.",
    sufferings: "Near-total blindness from trachoma in his last years; the stigmata's open wounds, which bled for the rest of his life; extreme fasting that likely damaged his health permanently.",
    death: "Died at sunset on 3 October 1226 at the Porziuncola, having asked to be laid naked on the bare earth as he had lived; Brother Elias announced the death publicly only after concealing the stigmata had become impossible.",
