    conversion: "The convalescence at Loyola after Pamplona — noticing that daydreams of chivalry felt hollow afterward while daydreams of imitating the saints left lasting peace. That single observation became the founding insight of Ignatian discernment.",

    anecdotes: [
      { title: "The double leg-breaking", text: "The leg set by the Pamplona surgeons healed with a bone protruding and shortened — unacceptable to the still-vain young Íñigo, who insisted the surgeons saw off the protrusion and rebreak and stretch the leg, all without anaesthetic, so he could wear the fashionable tight hose of a courtier again.", source: "Ignatius's own dictated Autobiography", legend: false },
      { title: "The vigil at Montserrat", text: "In imitation of a knight's vigil before dubbing, Ignatius spent a night in prayer before the Black Madonna of Montserrat, then hung up his sword and dagger there and gave his fine clothes to a poor man, taking on a beggar's sackcloth instead.", source: "Ignatius's own Autobiography", legend: false },
      { title: "Back to school with children", text: "In his thirties, having realized his lack of education was a real obstacle, Ignatius sat in a Barcelona grammar class among boys a third his age to learn Latin from scratch, enduring their mockery without complaint.", source: "Ignatius's own Autobiography", legend: false },
      { title: "The Moor and the mule", text: "Riding with a Moor who denied the perpetual virginity of Mary, Ignatius, still hot-tempered from his soldiering days, considered chasing the man down and stabbing him for the insult, then let his mule choose the road at a fork instead of deciding himself — resolving to kill the man only if the mule took the same road. It took the other road, and Ignatius let the matter go.", source: "Ignatius's own Autobiography", legend: false },
      { title: "The vision at La Storta", text: "Just outside Rome in 1537, praying in a small chapel at La Storta with his first companions, Ignatius said he saw God the Father place him with Christ, who was carrying his cross, and heard the Father say, 'I will be favourable to you in Rome.' He took it as confirmation of the Society's mission and its bond to the papacy.", source: "Ignatius's own account, recorded by his companions Laínez and Faber", legend: false },
      { title: "Weeping over the Mass", text: "In his later years saying Mass, Ignatius was reported to weep so uncontrollably at the consecration that he sometimes had to pause for long stretches, and his secretaries noted he eventually asked to say Mass privately and at length rather than in community, so as not to delay the other priests.", source: "Testimony collected in the canonization process", legend: false },
      { title: "\"If God were to destroy the Society\"", text: "Asked by a companion how he would feel if the Society of Jesus, still young and fragile, were suddenly suppressed, Ignatius reportedly said that a quarter of an hour of prayer would be enough to restore his peace — a remark later generations of Jesuits took as the classic statement of his own detachment from even his life's greatest work.", source: "Attributed in early Jesuit oral tradition and repeated in later biographies; not found verbatim in his own writings", legend: true },
    ],

