    conversion: "Less a single conversion than a decisive break: revulsion at the vice of student Rome sent him first to Enfide and then to total solitude at Subiaco, from which his whole later life as a founder grew.",

    anecdotes: [
      { title: "The shattered cup", text: "Monks at Vicovaro, resenting the strict abbot they had themselves begged for, put poison in his wine. Benedict made the sign of the cross over the cup before drinking, as was his custom, and the vessel shattered as though struck by a stone.", source: "Gregory the Great, Dialogues, Book II", legend: true },
      { title: "The raven and the poisoned bread", text: "The priest Florentius, jealous of Benedict's growing reputation, sent him a loaf of bread laced with poison. Benedict, recognizing the danger, ordered a raven that regularly visited him to carry the loaf far away and drop it where no one would find it — which it did, after some hesitation and evident reluctance to leave its master's presence.", source: "Gregory the Great, Dialogues, Book II", legend: true },
      { title: "The night of the storm", text: "At their final annual meeting, Scholastica begged Benedict to stay the night talking of heaven; when he refused, insisting on his own rule of returning to the monastery before dark, she put her head in her hands and prayed — and a storm broke so violent that Benedict could not leave. 'God forgive you, sister,' he said. 'What have you done?' 'I asked you and you would not listen,' she answered. 'I asked my Lord, and he has listened.'", source: "Gregory the Great, Dialogues, Book II", legend: true },
      { title: "Placid saved from the lake", text: "A young monk, Placid, went to fetch water and was swept into the lake by the current. Benedict, praying in his cell, saw this in spirit and sent Maurus running to help; Maurus, without noticing, walked out onto the surface of the water, pulled Placid out by the hair, and only realized once safely on land that he had been walking on water — a feat he attributed entirely to Benedict's command and prayer, not his own merit.", source: "Gregory the Great, Dialogues, Book II", legend: true },
      { title: "The broken sieve restored", text: "A devout woman lent Scholastica's nurse a glass sieve, which the nurse then accidentally broke into two pieces, leaving her in tears at having ruined a borrowed object. Benedict, moved by her distress, prayed over the two broken halves, and they were found rejoined so seamlessly that no crack could be seen — a story Gregory the Great records as the first sign of Benedict's holiness to become locally known.", source: "Gregory the Great, Dialogues, Book II", legend: true },
      { title: "Rebuking King Totila", text: "The Ostrogothic king Totila, testing whether Benedict truly had the gift of prophecy, sent an attendant dressed in royal robes in his place; Benedict immediately saw through the disguise and told the man to take off clothes that were not his. Totila then came in person, and Benedict rebuked him for his cruelties and predicted, accurately, that he would take Rome, cross the sea, reign nine more years, and then die.", source: "Gregory the Great, Dialogues, Book II", legend: true },
      { title: "The world in a ray of light", text: "Near the end of his life, rising before dawn to pray at his window, Benedict saw the whole world gathered up as if in a single ray of sunlight — a vision he described to his disciple Servandus, who was with him, and which Gregory the Great presents as a foretaste of how, in God's light, all creation appears small.", source: "Gregory the Great, Dialogues, Book II", legend: true },
    ],

