    conversion: "Not a conversion but a second, more radical call within an already religious life — 'the call within a call' of 10 September 1946, redirecting an already-professed nun from convent teaching to direct, unenclosed work among the destitute poor.",

    anecdotes: [
      { title: "The call within a call", text: "On a train to a retreat in Darjeeling on 10 September 1946, Mother Teresa said she received an unmistakable interior call from Christ to leave the convent and work among the poorest of the poor, living among them rather than serving them from within an institution — an experience she treated as decisive and never subsequently doubted.", source: "Her own later testimony and correspondence, and the 1946 date preserved in Missionaries of Charity tradition", legend: false },
      { title: "The Nobel banquet", text: "Accepting the Nobel Peace Prize in Oslo in 1979, Mother Teresa asked that the customary state banquet held in the laureate's honour be cancelled, and that its funds — reportedly around $6,000 — be given to the poor instead, saying she did not need it and the poor of Calcutta did.", source: "Widely documented contemporary press coverage of the 1979 Nobel ceremony", legend: false },
      { title: "\"Do small things with great love\"", text: "Asked repeatedly how ordinary people, without her resources or vocation, could imitate her work, Mother Teresa's consistent answer was some version of 'we can do no great things, only small things with great love' — a phrase now inseparably associated with her.", source: "Widely attributed in her public talks and interviews", legend: false },
      { title: "The hidden darkness", text: "For nearly fifty years, Mother Teresa privately experienced what she described to her spiritual directors as an absence of God's felt presence — writing at one point that heaven, for her, 'means nothing' and that she felt only emptiness where others found consolation — while continuing publicly to teach and radiate joy. She asked that most of this correspondence be destroyed; it survived largely because her director, Fr. Neuner, kept copies, and it was published posthumously with Church permission.", source: "Come Be My Light (2007), ed. Fr. Brian Kolodiejchuk, M.C., drawing on her private letters", legend: false },
      { title: "Picking up the dying from the street", text: "In the earliest days of her work, before any institution existed, Mother Teresa reportedly carried a dying woman found on a Calcutta street, being eaten by insects, to a hospital that initially refused to admit her because she could not pay — an episode she cited as the direct impetus for opening Nirmal Hriday shortly after.", source: "Recounted in early biographical accounts drawing on her own telling and that of early companions", legend: true },
      { title: "Learning basic nursing in Patna", text: "Before beginning her street ministry, Mother Teresa spent several months in 1948 with the Medical Mission Sisters in Patna acquiring rudimentary nursing skills — insisting on the most basic, practical training rather than any theoretical preparation, since she intended to begin work immediately among people with no other access to care.", source: "Documented in early biographical accounts of the founding period", legend: false },
      { title: "The Missionaries of Charity's single 'luxury'", text: "Visitors often remarked on the extreme simplicity of the Missionaries of Charity's own convents — two saris each, sleeping on thin mats, no fans even in Calcutta's heat — which Mother Teresa insisted on so that the sisters would live materially closer to the poor they served rather than above them.", source: "Widely documented in visitor accounts and the congregation's own constitutions", legend: false },
    ],

