  dailyPractice: {
    rhythm: "The Missionaries of Charity's strict horarium: rising at 4:30 a.m. for an hour of meditation before Mass and Communion, an hour of Eucharistic Adoration built into the day, fixed community prayers at set hours, then direct physical service to the poorest for most of the working day, all sustained — as her private letters revealed — through decades of felt spiritual darkness rather than consolation.",
    practices: [
      { name: "Rising at 4:30 a.m. for meditation", detail: "The Missionaries of Charity day began with an hour of silent meditation before the community Mass, a schedule Mother Teresa herself kept without exception.", source: "The Missionaries of Charity's own published Constitutions and community horarium", legend: false },
      { name: "Daily Mass and Communion", detail: "Treated as the non-negotiable centre of the day, before any of the active work began.", source: "MC Constitutions", legend: false },
      { name: "An hour of Eucharistic Adoration", detail: "Instituted as a fixed daily practice for the whole community, alongside the active work — contemplation and direct service to the poor treated as inseparable rather than competing.", source: "MC Constitutions and community practice", legend: false },
      { name: "Direct, hands-on service to the poorest", detail: "Most of the working day given to physically caring for the dying, abandoned, and destitute personally rather than administratively — considered itself a form of prayer ('touching Christ in his distressing disguise'), not separate from it.", source: "Her own frequently repeated teaching and the MC's daily practice", legend: false },
      { name: "Continuing the full routine through felt spiritual darkness", detail: "For nearly fifty years she kept this entire daily structure — prayer, Adoration, active service — without the felt consolation most people associate with a rich prayer life, treating fidelity to the routine itself, not the feeling, as the substance of her prayer.", source: "Come Be My Light (2007), her private correspondence", legend: false },
    ],
    forYou: "The single most important lesson from Mother Teresa's daily practice may be this: she kept the exact same prayer schedule for nearly fifty years while feeling almost nothing from it. If your own prayer time feels dry or empty, her example says that's not evidence it isn't working — the routine itself, kept faithfully regardless of how it feels, was precisely her own path to holiness. Also concrete and copyable: she treated service to other people as a form of prayer in itself, not a break from 'real' spiritual time — worth remembering on days when formal prayer and practical duties feel like they're competing for the same hour.",
  },
